-------------------------------------------------------------
  1. THE BIG PICTURE — What happens when you run the program
-------------------------------------------------------------

When you run:
    python3 a_maze_ing.py

The program goes through 5 stages in order:

    STAGE 1 — Read the config file (config.txt)
        Parse WIDTH, HEIGHT, ENTRY, EXIT, OUTPUT_FILE, PERFECT.
        If anything is wrong, print an error and stop cleanly.

    STAGE 2 — Generate the maze
        Build a 2D grid where every cell starts as a fully
        walled box.  Then carve passages between cells using
        the DFS algorithm until every reachable cell is
        connected.  The "42" pattern cells are placed BEFORE
        this step so the algorithm never touches them.

    STAGE 3 — Solve the maze
        Run BFS (Breadth-First Search) from ENTRY to EXIT
        to find the shortest possible path through the maze.

    STAGE 4 — Write the output file
        Save the maze + entry + exit + path to maze.txt
        (or whatever OUTPUT_FILE says) in the format Agnes's
        code knows how to read.

    STAGE 5 — Display + interactive menu
        Convert the maze into the hex-string format Agnes's
        display expects, draw it on screen, then enter a loop
        where the user can regenerate, toggle the path, change
        colours, or quit.

The whole journey of data:

    config.txt
        |
        v
    config_parser.py  ->  config dict (WIDTH, HEIGHT, etc.)
        |
        v
    mazegen/generator.py  ->  cells  (2D grid of integers)
        |
        v
    mazegen/solver.py     ->  path   (list of directions)
        |
        v
    mazegen/writer.py     ->  maze.txt  (the output file)
        |
        v
    mazegen/writer.py (encode_maze)  ->  hex_grid (for display)
        |
        v
    display.py (Agnes)    ->  terminal output
        |
        v
    menu.py               ->  user interaction loop


-------------------------------------------------------------
  2. THE CORE DATA STRUCTURE — How a cell is stored
-------------------------------------------------------------

Every cell in the maze is stored as a single integer between
0 and 15.  That integer uses 4 bits, one per wall direction:

    Bit 0  (value 1)  =  NORTH wall
    Bit 1  (value 2)  =  EAST wall
    Bit 2  (value 4)  =  SOUTH wall
    Bit 3  (value 8)  =  WEST wall

    If the bit is 1, the wall EXISTS (is closed / solid).
    If the bit is 0, the wall is ABSENT (a passage exists).

Examples:
    0xF  =  15  =  1111 in binary  =  all 4 walls closed
    0x0  =   0  =  0000 in binary  =  no walls (fully open)
    0x5  =   5  =  0101 in binary  =  north + south walls only

The entire maze is a 2D list of these integers:
    cells[row][col]  ->  one integer for that cell

This is YOUR internal format.  Agnes never sees integers —
she gets single hex characters (0-F) which she converts back
using decode_cell() in file_reader.py.  The conversion is:
    integer 15  ->  hex character 'F'
    integer  5  ->  hex character '5'
    integer  0  ->  hex character '0'


