-------------------------------------------------------------
  11. menu.py — THE INTERACTIVE LOOP
-------------------------------------------------------------

    This file calls Agnes's display code and your generation
    code together to power the interactive session.

    run_menu(initial_cells, config)
    --------------------------------
    The outer loop.  Runs forever until the user picks "Quit".

    State it tracks:
        cells       — the current maze (can be replaced on
                      regenerate).
        show_path   — boolean toggle for showing the solution.
        wall_color  — ANSI escape code string for wall colour
                      (empty string = default terminal colour).

    Each iteration:
        1. Clear the screen.
        2. Call _render() to draw the maze.
        3. Call _print_menu() to show the options.
        4. Wait for user input.
        5. Act on it:
            '1' → call _regenerate() to get new cells
            '2' → flip show_path True/False
            '3' → call _pick_color() to get a new wall_color
            '4' → break out of the loop (quit)
            anything else → do nothing, just redraw

    _regenerate(config)
    -------------------
    Calls generate_maze() and solve_maze() with the same config
    values, writes the new maze to the output file, and returns
    the new cells grid.  The menu loop replaces its cells
    variable with the returned value.

    _render(cells, config, show_path, wall_color)
    ----------------------------------------------
    Step 1: Call encode_maze() to convert cells (integers) into
            the hex-string format Agnes's display expects.
    Step 2: If show_path is True, call solve_maze() again on the
            current cells to get the path directions, then call
            process_directions() (Agnes's function) to convert
            those directions into a list of (x,y) coordinates.
            If show_path is False, coord_path stays empty.
    Step 3: Call Agnes's create_grid() with the hex grid,
            dimensions, entry, exit, path coordinates, and the
            chosen wall colour.

    _print_menu(show_path, wall_color)
    -----------------------------------
    Prints the menu box to the terminal.
    Shows the current state of the path toggle (ON/OFF) and the
    currently selected wall colour name inline in the menu.

    _pick_color()
    -------------
    Clears the screen and shows a numbered list of colour choices.
    Reads the user's number, looks up the matching ANSI escape
    code from the _WALL_COLORS list, and returns it.
    Falls back to the default (empty string) on bad input.

    _clear()
    --------
    Clears the terminal.  Uses the right command for the OS
    ('clear' on Linux/Mac, 'cls' on Windows).


-------------------------------------------------------------
  12. HOW THE TWO HALVES CONNECT
-------------------------------------------------------------

Your code and Agnes's code meet at two points:

    POINT A — The output file (maze.txt)
        You write it in writer.py.
        Agnes reads it in file_reader.py.
        The agreed format (hex grid + blank line + entry +
        exit + path) is the contract between the two halves.

    POINT B — The encode_maze() function
        Instead of going through the file, menu.py calls
        encode_maze() to convert your integer grid directly
        into the hex-string format Agnes's create_grid() needs.
        This avoids a pointless file round-trip during display.

    Agnes's functions you call from menu.py:
        encode_maze()         → from your writer.py
        create_grid()         → Agnes's renderer
        process_directions()  → Agnes's path coordinate builder


